'''vibraculum''' (vī-brak′ū-lum), ''n.''; pl. ''vibracula''
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|''v'', four Vibracula of the Polyzoarium of a Polyzoan (''Scrupocellaria ferox''); ''a'', articulation of the base of one of them. (Magnified.)}}}}
(-lä̤). [NL., < L. ''vibrare'', shake, agitate: see ''vibrate''.] One of the long filamentous or flagelliform appendages of the cells or ectocysts of many polyzoans, usually articulated with short dilated processes of the ectocyst, and executing constant lashing movements by the contraction of muscles contained in their dilated bases; a flabellarium. {{smaller|These lashing organs are highly characteristic, like the snapping or beak-like organs with which some polyzoans are also provided. See ''avicularium''.}}

'''vibrant''' (vī′brant), ''a.'' [< F. ''vibrant'' = Sp. Pg. It. ''vibrante'', < L. ''vibran(t-)s'', ppr. of ''vibrare'', vibrate: see ''vibrate''.] '''1.''' Vibrating; agitated; specifically, vibrating so as to produce sound: as, a ''vibrant'' string.
{{smaller block|Each man has his private barometer of hope, the mercury in which is more or less sensitive, and the opinion ''vibrant'' with its rise or fall. {{right|''Lowell'', Fireside Travels, p. 119.}}}}
